{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFfDgFYnfbbLJE9QnHhMsk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need to provide a comprehensive answer. Let me compile this into a clear report.\n\n## Summary\n\nI've thoroughly examined the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, and testing patterns across multiple cmd packages. Here are my findings:\n\n### 1. DiffTest Struct Fields\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5277/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 46-55):\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**Key field behaviors:**\n\n- **Stdin** (line 35-36): `nil` means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil slice `[]byte{}` also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is immediately closed.\n- **Env** (lines 39-42): `nil` means both binaries inherit the test process environment with `LC_ALL=C` applied as a default override. When non-nil, KEY=VALUE pairs are merged: matching keys override, new keys are added.\n- **ExitCode** (line 35): Defaults to 0 when left as zero value. This is explicitly compared between Go and reference binaries.\n- **Normalize** (line 44-45): Slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison.\n- **ExpectedFiles** (line 54): Map of relative paths to expected file contents, checked after binary execution.\n\n### 2. RunDiffTests Exit Code Comparison\n\n**YES - RunDiffTests explicitly compares exit codes.** This is verified in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5277/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`:\n\nLines 176-185 (`compareOutputs` function):\n```go\nfunc compareOutputs(t *testing.T, tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tstdoutMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stdout, refRes.stdout)\n\tstderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)\n\texitMatch := goRes.exitCode == refRes.exitCode  // <-- EXIT CODE COMPARISON\n\tif stdoutMatch && stderrMatch && exitMatch {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tt.Fatalf(\"%s\", formatDivergence(tc, goRes, refRes))\n}\n```\n\nLines 195-197 show exit codes are reported in divergence messages:\n```\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref exit:    %d\\n\", refRes.exitCode)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  exit:    %d\\n\", goRes.exitCode)\n```\n\nThe exit code is extracted via `extractResult` (lines 130-148) which handles both successful runs (exit 0) and `exec.ExitError` cases.\n\n### 3. R4 Test Patterns Across cmd/ Packages\n\nI found comprehensive R4 (exit codes, file errors, SIGPIPE) testing patterns:\n\n#### **cmd/cat/cat_test.go** (lines 60-112):\n- **File errors (R4 exit codes)**: TestMissingFile (lines 60-80) - expects exit 1 when a file is missing, with stderr containing the filename\n- **SIGPIPE handling (R5.4)**: TestSIGPIPE (lines 83-112) - tests that cat exits 0 when stdout is closed by downstream consumer (broken pipe scenario). Uses context timeout to detect hangs.\n\n#### **cmd/head/head_test.go** (lines 153-272):\n- **File errors (R4.2)**: Multiple tests with `ExitCode: 1, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{discardStderr}`:\n  - nonexistent-only (line 264)\n  - nonexistent-with-valid (line 240)\n  - valid-then-nonexistent (line 248)\n  - nonexistent-between-valid (line 256)\n- Uses `discardStderr` normalizer to ignore binary name/path differences in error messages while still verifying exit code\n\n#### **cmd/tee/tee_test.go** (lines 309-407):\n- **File write errors (R4)**: TestWriteErrorReadOnly (lines 309-343) - expects exit 1 when write fails to a read-only directory, verifies stdout was still produced, and checks stderr mentions the failed file\n- **Continues after errors**: TestWriteErrorContinuesWriting (lines 345-370) - verifies that after a write error, tee continues writing to remaining files and exits 1\n- **Broken stdout**: TestBrokenStdout (lines 372-378) - expects exit 1 when stdout pipe is broken\n- **SIGINT signal handling (R4)**: TestDiffSIGINTSignal (lines 391-408) - compares exit codes and behavior between Go and reference binaries when receiving SIGINT\n\n#### **cmd/wc/wc_test.go** (lines 655-724):\n- **File errors (R6.2)**: Multiple tests with `ExitCode: 1, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeBinaryName, normalizeErrCase}`:\n  - nonexistent_file_only (line 675)\n  - nonexistent_then_valid (line 683)\n  - valid_then_nonexistent (line 691)\n  - nonexistent_between_valid (line 699)\n  - Uses both binary name normalization and lowercase normalization for error message comparison\n\n#### **cmd/fold/fold_test.go** (lines 326-371):\n- **Exit codes and file errors (R4.1-R4.2)**: TestDiffExitCodes function:\n  - success_exit_0 (line 340) - verifies exit 0 on success\n  - nonexistent_file_exit_1 (line 346) - expects exit 1 with stderr normalization\n  - nonexistent_with_valid_continues (line 354) - continues processing remaining files\n  - valid_then_nonexistent (line 362) - tests file processing order\n\n### Key Testing Strategies for R4 Requirements\n\n1. **Exit Code Testing**: Set `ExitCode: 1` in DiffTest to verify both binaries exit with the same code when errors occur\n2. **Stderr Normalization**: Use `testutils.NormalizeFunc(func([]byte) []byte { return nil })` to discard stderr entirely, or normalize error messages to focus on exit code matching\n3. **SIGPIPE Handling**: Use custom test code with `context.WithTimeout`, broken pipes, and process signal handling (not integrated into DiffTest directly)\n4. **File Error Continuation**: Use multiple file arguments with mixed valid/invalid files to verify the binary continues processing and exits 1\n5. **Error Message Normalization**: For regex-based normalization of binary names and message casing, use `testutils.NormalizeFunc` composition\n\nThe testing framework is robust for R4 requirements and is being actively used across the codebase."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba4d68e-6e98-457c-a01f-730b17272aed","uuid":"40632433-e9d8-47a9-a631-836d32601362","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:49:11.645Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests function in pkg/testutils. Specifically:\n1. What fields does DiffTest have? (especially for exit codes, env vars, args, stdin)\n2. Does RunDiffTests compare exit codes between the Go and reference binaries?\n3. How do other cmd/ packages test R4.x requirements (exit codes, file errors, SIGPIPE)?\n\nSearch in:\n- pkg/testutils/ for the DiffTest struct definition and RunDiffTests implementation\n- Any cmd/*/unexpand_test.go or cmd/*_test.go files that test exit codes or file-not-found scenarios\n\nBe thorough - check multiple cmd/ directories for R4 test patterns.","agentId":"a7003f15a74493944","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need to provide a comprehensive answer. Let me compile this into a clear report.\n\n## Summary\n\nI've thoroughly examined the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, and testing patterns across multiple cmd packages. Here are my findings:\n\n### 1. DiffTest Struct Fields\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5277/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 46-55):\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**Key field behaviors:**\n\n- **Stdin** (line 35-36): `nil` means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil slice `[]byte{}` also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is im